// core:slices — ordering, searching and rearranging a run of anything.
//
//     import "core:slices"
//
//     [6]i32 ns = [5, 3, 9, 1, 7, 2]
//     slices.sort_ascending(ns[:])
//     (i64 at, bool found) = slices.find_sorted(ns[:], 7)
//
// **There is no array library to write beside this one, and that is the whole
// shape of the package.** `[N]T` windows to a `[]T` with `v[:]`, and a
// `ref []T` *lends* one — so a function taking `[]$T` already serves a fixed
// array, a borrowed window and an owned run, with nothing written at the call
// beyond the window that was always going to be there. One package, three
// container kinds, no conversions.
//
// ## What an importer pays, measured
//
// The emitter writes **every concrete function of every live package** whether
// it is called or not; its one exemption is an uncalled template. Nearly
// everything here binds a `$`, so nearly everything is demand-driven, and the
// three numbers below are what that buys. Measured against a 12-line
// empty-program baseline:
//
//     import and call nothing        12 lines      the package is pruned whole
//     call `contains` only           98 lines      contains, index_of, depth_budget
//     call `sort_ascending` only    292 lines      that sort and its five helpers
//
// The first line is the pruner rather than this package: *a package no root can
// reach contributes nothing at all*. The interesting one is the third — sorting
// brings the sort, and not the comparator sort beside it, not the equality
// half, not the predicates, not the searches.
//
// **`depth_budget` is the one concrete function here, and it is the whole floor
// an importer pays** — eight lines, present in the second measurement above
// even though `contains` has no use for it. It is left concrete rather than
// contorted into a template to make the headline rounder: eight lines is a fair
// price for a function that reads `i64 -> i64`, and stating the floor is worth
// more than hiding it.
//
// The sharpened half of the rule shapes everything else: **a template is free
// only when nothing concrete in its own package calls it.** `depth_budget`
// calls no template, so nothing is dragged in behind it.
//
// ## No `foreign` block, no import, no allocation
//
// This package emits no `#include`, so a program on a target whose OS is
// `#NONE` may use every line of it. Nothing here allocates: everything sorts,
// searches and rearranges **in place**, or writes into a slice the caller
// already owns. A stable sort needs O(n) scratch and is therefore not here —
// when it lands it takes an allocator, written last and mandatory, the way
// `#format` already settles that question.
//
// It imports nothing, `core:strings` included, and that is worth one sentence
// because ordering text is the obvious thing to want. `<` on a `string` is
// deliberately undefined — the language declines to have an opinion about
// collation — and `core:strings` already owns one. So the answer is the
// comparator you were going to pass anyway:
//
//     slices.sort(names, strings.before)
//
// which needs no dependency here and keeps the collation decision in the one
// package that has made it.
//
// ## Three doors to an ordering, and why not one
//
// A template **can** ask whether `T` is ordered now — `where T has <` — but the
// functions below predate it and keep their domains: the category-bound ones
// take `[]T where T is scalar`, and widening them to every type `<` applies to (a `char8`,
// an enum, a pointer, a `#c` scalar) is a change to what they accept, not a
// respelling. A comparator stays the universal mechanism. What is sugar over
// it:
//
//   * `sort(xs, less)` — universal, one instantiation per element type;
//   * `sort_ascending(xs)` — over `[]T where T is scalar`, whose category carries the six
//     comparisons, so the commonest case needs no comparator at all;
//   * `slices.sort(xs, strings.before)` — for text, above.
//
// A `less` is a **strict weak ordering**: `less(a, b)` is true exactly when `a`
// sorts before `b`, and `less(a, a)` must be false. A comparator that says an
// element precedes itself makes the partition below run past the end of the
// slice, and nothing here checks it — it is the one thing a caller owes.
package slices

// ── swapping and moving ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Exchange two elements. The right-hand side of a parenthesised place list is
// materialised before any store, which is what makes this a swap and not two
// assignments that lose a value.
public void swap([]$T xs, i64 i, i64 j):
    (xs[i], xs[j]) = (xs[j], xs[i])
    return

// Reverse in place.
public void reverse([]$T xs):
    i64 lo = 0
    i64 hi = #len(xs) - 1
    for lo < hi:
        swap(xs, lo, hi)
        lo = lo + 1
        hi = hi - 1
    return

// Rotate left by `n`, so the element at `n` becomes the first. A negative `n`
// rotates right, and `n` past the length wraps — both because the count is
// reduced modulo the length before anything moves, which makes the function
// total for every `n` a caller can produce.
//
// Three reversals and no scratch: the classic, and the reason this needs no
// allocator where a rotate-by-copy would.
public void rotate([]$T xs, i64 n):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count <= 1:
        return
    i64 by = n % count
    if by < 0:
        by = by + count
    if by == 0:
        return
    reverse(xs[0..<by])
    reverse(xs[by..<count])
    reverse(xs)
    return

// Set every element to `v`.
public void fill([]$T xs, T v):
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        xs[i] = v
    return

// Copy `from` into `into`, and answer how many elements moved — the shorter of
// the two lengths, so neither side has to be measured by the caller.
//
// **The two must not overlap.** Nothing here checks it and the loop runs
// forwards, so a copy into a window that starts later inside the same run
// smears its first element. Use `rotate` for a shift within one run.
public i64 copy_into([]$T from, []T into):
    i64 count = #len(from)
    i64 room = #len(into)
    if room < count:
        count = room
    for i in i64(0)..<count:
        into[i] = from[i]
    return count

// ── equality ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each of these carries `where #has_eq(T)`: `==` applies to `T`, which is
// exactly what their bodies ask of an element, and the word is answered by the
// same rule the operator is, so a `T` these admit is a `T` whose `xs[i] == probe`
// compiles. A float slice is admitted — `==` is IEEE equality, so a NaN probe is
// never found. Stating the domain is what makes a call over elements with no
// `==`, such as maps, fail at the **call**, naming it, rather than failing
// inside a body the caller did not write.

// The first index holding `probe`, or -1.
public i64 index_of([]$T xs, T probe) where #has_eq(T):
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if xs[i] == probe:
            return i
    return 0 - 1

// The last index holding `probe`, or -1.
public i64 last_index_of([]$T xs, T probe) where #has_eq(T):
    i64 i = #len(xs) - 1
    for i >= 0:
        if xs[i] == probe:
            return i
        i = i - 1
    return 0 - 1

// Whether `probe` is anywhere in `xs`.
public bool contains([]$T xs, T probe) where #has_eq(T):
    return index_of(xs, probe) >= 0

// How many elements equal `probe`.
public i64 count_of([]$T xs, T probe) where #has_eq(T):
    i64 seen = 0
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if xs[i] == probe:
            seen = seen + 1
    return seen

// Whether two runs hold the same elements in the same order. Different lengths
// are never equal, and two empty runs are.
public bool equal([]$T a, []T b) where #has_eq(T):
    i64 count = #len(a)
    i64 other = #len(b)
    if count != other:
        return false
    for i in i64(0)..<count:
        if a[i] != b[i]:
            return false
    return true

// Whether `xs` begins with `prefix`.
public bool starts_with([]$T xs, []T prefix) where #has_eq(T):
    i64 count = #len(prefix)
    i64 room = #len(xs)
    if count > room:
        return false
    return equal(xs[0..<count], prefix)

// Collapse runs of adjacent equal elements to one, in place, and answer the new
// length. The elements past it are left as they were and are the caller's to
// window off: `xs[0..<slices.unique(xs)]`.
//
// **Adjacent, not global** — this is `uniq` and not a set, so a caller who wants
// every duplicate gone sorts first. Saying so is the whole documentation, and
// making it allocate a set instead would be a different function with a
// different cost.
public i64 unique([]$T xs) where #has_eq(T):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count <= 1:
        return count
    i64 kept = 1
    for i in i64(1)..<count:
        if xs[i] != xs[kept - 1]:
            xs[kept] = xs[i]
            kept = kept + 1
    return kept

// ── predicates ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Whether every element satisfies `pred`. True for an empty run, which is the
// convention every language with this function has and the one that makes
// `all(a) && all(b)` equal `all(a ++ b)`.
public bool all([]$T xs, bool(T) pred):
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if !pred(xs[i]):
            return false
    return true

// Whether any element satisfies `pred`. False for an empty run.
public bool any([]$T xs, bool(T) pred):
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if pred(xs[i]):
            return true
    return false

// Whether no element satisfies `pred`.
public bool none([]$T xs, bool(T) pred):
    return !any(xs, pred)

// How many elements satisfy `pred`.
public i64 count_if([]$T xs, bool(T) pred):
    i64 seen = 0
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if pred(xs[i]):
            seen = seen + 1
    return seen

// The first index satisfying `pred`, or -1.
public i64 find_if([]$T xs, bool(T) pred):
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if pred(xs[i]):
            return i
    return 0 - 1

// Move every element satisfying `pred` to the front, in place, and answer how
// many there are. **Not stable** — relative order within each group is not
// preserved, because a stable partition needs scratch and this needs none.
public i64 partition([]$T xs, bool(T) pred):
    i64 kept = 0
    for i in i64(0)..<#len(xs):
        if pred(xs[i]):
            swap(xs, i, kept)
            kept = kept + 1
    return kept

// ── ordering ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The index of the smallest element under `less`, or -1 for an empty run. The
// **first** such index when several compare equal, which is what makes it a
// stable choice and the reason the test is `less(xs[i], xs[at])` rather than
// `!less(xs[at], xs[i])`.
public i64 min_index([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count <= 0:
        return 0 - 1
    i64 at = 0
    for i in i64(1)..<count:
        if less(xs[i], xs[at]):
            at = i
    return at

// The index of the largest element under `less`, or -1. The first such index.
public i64 max_index([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count <= 0:
        return 0 - 1
    i64 at = 0
    for i in i64(1)..<count:
        if less(xs[at], xs[i]):
            at = i
    return at

// Whether `xs` is ordered under `less`. True for a run of fewer than two.
public bool is_sorted([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count < 2:
        return true
    for i in i64(1)..<count:
        if less(xs[i], xs[i - 1]):
            return false
    return true

// Sort in place under `less`.
//
// **Introsort**: quicksort with a median-of-three pivot, an insertion sort under
// `SMALL`, and a heapsort fallback once the recursion has gone deeper than
// `2·log2(n)`. That last part is what makes the worst case O(n log n) rather
// than O(n²), and it is not decoration — a plain quicksort's worst case is
// reachable from ordinary input, not only from an adversary, and a library
// whose sort degrades on sorted-then-appended data is a library people stop
// trusting.
//
// **It is not stable.** Equal elements may be reordered. A stable sort needs
// O(n) scratch, so it takes an allocator, and it is a separate function that is
// not written yet rather than a flag on this one.
public void sort([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count < 2:
        return
    introsort(xs, depth_budget(count), less)
    return

// Ascending, with no comparator at all.
//
// **The `scalar` category carries the six comparisons**, which is the whole of
// what an ordering needs, so this is the same algorithm with `<` written where
// the call would have been — and it costs a caller nothing to reach for. Every
// numeric width is one instantiation of one source.
public void sort_ascending([]$T xs) where T is scalar:
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count < 2:
        return
    intro_up(xs, depth_budget(count))
    return

// Descending. A separate template rather than a `bool` on the one above,
// because a flag tested inside the innermost comparison of a sort is a branch
// per comparison, and two instantiations of a template cost nothing when only
// one is called.
public void sort_descending([]$T xs) where T is scalar:
    sort_ascending(xs)
    reverse(xs)
    return

// ── searching a sorted run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The first index at which `probe` could be inserted with the order preserved —
// i.e. the first element not ordered before it. `xs` must be sorted under
// `less`; nothing here checks that, and on an unsorted run the answer is
// meaningless rather than wrong.
public i64 lower_bound([]$T xs, T probe, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 lo = 0
    i64 hi = #len(xs)
    for lo < hi:
        i64 mid = lo + ((hi - lo) / 2)
        if less(xs[mid], probe):
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid
    return lo

// The last index at which `probe` could be inserted — i.e. the first element
// `probe` is ordered before. `upper_bound - lower_bound` is how many copies of
// `probe` the run holds.
public i64 upper_bound([]$T xs, T probe, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 lo = 0
    i64 hi = #len(xs)
    for lo < hi:
        i64 mid = lo + ((hi - lo) / 2)
        if less(probe, xs[mid]):
            hi = mid
        else:
            lo = mid + 1
    return lo

// Find `probe` in a sorted run.
//
// **`at` is meaningful whether or not it was found**: on a hit it is where the
// element is, and on a miss it is where one would be inserted. That is what
// makes this useful for *building* a sorted run and not only for probing one,
// and it is why the answer is a pair rather than an index with -1 for absent.
//
// Equality is asked as `!less(probe, xs[at])` and not with `==`, so the element
// type needs no equality of its own — the ordering is the only thing this
// template requires, which is what lets it serve a `T` that has no `#has_eq`.
public (i64 at, bool found) binary_search([]$T xs, T probe, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 at = lower_bound(xs, probe, less)
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if at < count && !less(probe, xs[at]):
        return (at, true)
    return (at, false)

// The same, over a run sorted ascending, with no comparator.
public (i64 at, bool found) find_sorted([]$T xs, T probe) where T is scalar:
    i64 lo = 0
    i64 hi = #len(xs)
    for lo < hi:
        i64 mid = lo + ((hi - lo) / 2)
        if xs[mid] < probe:
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if lo < count && !(probe < xs[lo]):
        return (lo, true)
    return (lo, false)

// ── the machinery ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Everything below is private and every one of it is a template, so an importer
// that never sorts carries none of it.

// A run shorter than this is insertion-sorted, which beats quicksort's
// bookkeeping at small sizes and is where the recursion stops.
private const i64 SMALL = 16

// `2 · floor(log2(n))`, the depth at which introsort gives up on quicksort.
//
// `#clz` answers how many leading zero bits a value has, so `63 - #clz(n)` is
// `floor(log2(n))` for a `u64` — the bit verb doing the one job it is for. It is
// asked of an unsigned value because a bit count is a question about a *bit
// pattern* and C admits three representations of a negative one; `n` is a length
// and cannot be negative, so the conversion is free of consequence.
private i64 depth_budget(i64 n):
    if n < 2:
        return 1
    i64 levels = 63 - #clz(u64(n))
    return 2 * levels

// ── the comparator-driven half ──────────────────────────────────────────────

private void introsort([]$T xs, i64 budget, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count < 2:
        return
    if count <= SMALL:
        insertion(xs, less)
        return
    if budget <= 0:
        heapsort(xs, less)
        return
    i64 cut = partition_at(xs, less)
    introsort(xs[0..<cut], budget - 1, less)
    introsort(xs[cut + 1..<count], budget - 1, less)
    return

private void insertion([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    for i in i64(1)..<count:
        T key = xs[i]
        i64 j = i - 1
        for j >= 0 && less(key, xs[j]):
            xs[j + 1] = xs[j]
            j = j - 1
        xs[j + 1] = key
    return

// Median of first, middle and last, moved to the end, then Lomuto. The median
// is what keeps an already-sorted run off quicksort's worst case, which is the
// input a naive last-element pivot is worst on and the one real programs hand
// it most often.
private i64 partition_at([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    i64 last = count - 1
    i64 mid = count / 2
    if less(xs[mid], xs[0]):
        swap(xs, 0, mid)
    if less(xs[last], xs[0]):
        swap(xs, 0, last)
    if less(xs[last], xs[mid]):
        swap(xs, mid, last)
    swap(xs, mid, last)
    T pivot = xs[last]
    i64 store = 0
    for i in i64(0)..<last:
        if less(xs[i], pivot):
            swap(xs, i, store)
            store = store + 1
    swap(xs, store, last)
    return store

private void heapsort([]$T xs, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    i64 start = count / 2
    i64 i = start - 1
    for i >= 0:
        sift(xs, i, count, less)
        i = i - 1
    i64 end = count - 1
    for end > 0:
        swap(xs, 0, end)
        sift(xs, 0, end, less)
        end = end - 1
    return

private void sift([]$T xs, i64 root, i64 end, bool(T, T) less):
    i64 at = root
    for:
        i64 child = (2 * at) + 1
        if child >= end:
            return
        if child + 1 < end && less(xs[child], xs[child + 1]):
            child = child + 1
        if !less(xs[at], xs[child]):
            return
        swap(xs, at, child)
        at = child

// ── the comparator-free half ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The same three algorithms with `<` written where the call was. They are
// duplicated rather than shared, and that is the honest cost of the category
// binding: a `scalar` has the comparisons but no way to name one as a value, so
// there is no comparator to hand `introsort` that would make these one source.
// What is bought is that `sort_ascending(xs)` needs nothing at the call.

private void intro_up([]$T xs, i64 budget) where T is scalar:
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    if count < 2:
        return
    if count <= SMALL:
        insertion_up(xs)
        return
    if budget <= 0:
        heapsort_up(xs)
        return
    i64 cut = partition_up(xs)
    intro_up(xs[0..<cut], budget - 1)
    intro_up(xs[cut + 1..<count], budget - 1)
    return

private void insertion_up([]$T xs) where T is scalar:
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    for i in i64(1)..<count:
        T key = xs[i]
        i64 j = i - 1
        for j >= 0 && key < xs[j]:
            xs[j + 1] = xs[j]
            j = j - 1
        xs[j + 1] = key
    return

private i64 partition_up([]$T xs) where T is scalar:
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    i64 last = count - 1
    i64 mid = count / 2
    if xs[mid] < xs[0]:
        swap(xs, 0, mid)
    if xs[last] < xs[0]:
        swap(xs, 0, last)
    if xs[last] < xs[mid]:
        swap(xs, mid, last)
    swap(xs, mid, last)
    T pivot = xs[last]
    i64 store = 0
    for i in i64(0)..<last:
        if xs[i] < pivot:
            swap(xs, i, store)
            store = store + 1
    swap(xs, store, last)
    return store

private void heapsort_up([]$T xs) where T is scalar:
    i64 count = #len(xs)
    i64 start = count / 2
    i64 i = start - 1
    for i >= 0:
        sift_up(xs, i, count)
        i = i - 1
    i64 end = count - 1
    for end > 0:
        swap(xs, 0, end)
        sift_up(xs, 0, end)
        end = end - 1
    return

private void sift_up([]$T xs, i64 root, i64 end) where T is scalar:
    i64 at = root
    for:
        i64 child = (2 * at) + 1
        if child >= end:
            return
        if child + 1 < end && xs[child] < xs[child + 1]:
            child = child + 1
        if !(xs[at] < xs[child]):
            return
        swap(xs, at, child)
        at = child
